\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every fixed deletion probability $q\in(0,1)$ and every fixed positive reconstruction success probability, must worst-case binary length-$n$ deletion-trace reconstruction use at least $n^{c\log\log n}$ samples for some $c>0$ and all sufficiently large $n$?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For each fixed deletion probability $q\in(0,1)$ and each fixed positive reconstruction success probability, is worst-case reconstruction of binary strings of length $n$ from deletion traces required to use at least $n^{c\log\log n}$ samples, for some $c>0$ and every sufficiently large $n$?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://afonsobandeira.wordpress.com/2015/11/27/10l42grouptesting/}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/quantitative-lower-bounds-for-trace-reconstruction-September-24-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

